1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
Free worldwide wireless energy.

2
00:00:05,100 --> 00:00:10,940
Death rays, earthquake machines, remote control,

3
00:00:11,040 --> 00:00:16,040
and green power, all from the fertile mind of the genius

4
00:00:16,140 --> 00:00:19,060
who electrified the world.

5
00:00:19,160 --> 00:00:25,060
Now, Nikola Tesla's mad electricity on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
He was a true American hero, but after a while, people forgot about what he did.

7
00:00:45,360 --> 00:00:48,980
I believe that he was the most important inventor in human history.

8
00:00:49,580 --> 00:00:53,380
Some of his neighbors did think he was absolutely crazy, and they thought he was a danger to their community.

9
00:00:54,160 --> 00:00:57,860
I think that he had a different version of what the future would look like.

10
00:00:57,861 --> 00:01:00,360
He's responsible for the 20th century.

11
00:01:08,240 --> 00:01:13,200
For the power to change night into day,

12
00:01:13,300 --> 00:01:15,680
thank Nikola Tesla.

13
00:01:15,780 --> 00:01:19,020
It's clear there are so many aspects of modern life,

14
00:01:19,120 --> 00:01:22,180
everyday life, that is directly influenced or created

15
00:01:22,280 --> 00:01:23,220
by Nikola Tesla.

16
00:01:25,280 --> 00:01:27,860
His alternating current was the same.

17
00:01:27,861 --> 00:01:31,100
power system paved the way for all of our modern electric

18
00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:32,700
conveniences.

19
00:01:32,800 --> 00:01:36,660
He's basically responsible for our entire system

20
00:01:36,760 --> 00:01:40,400
of electricity, our entire modern world.

21
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:42,680
But tragically, in later years,

22
00:01:42,780 --> 00:01:46,920
his brilliance, responsible for more than 200 patents,

23
00:01:47,020 --> 00:01:50,320
was overshadowed by what some considered madness.

24
00:01:50,420 --> 00:01:53,880
He was often seen as sort of the mad scientist who

25
00:01:53,980 --> 00:01:57,820
was out of control and would destroy the world when he

26
00:01:57,860 --> 00:02:03,440
He really wanted to improve the lot of humanity.

27
00:02:03,540 --> 00:02:06,140
Because of Tesla, a turn of the switch

28
00:02:06,240 --> 00:02:09,240
brings power to our fingertips.

29
00:02:09,340 --> 00:02:11,380
Yet the same intellect fanatically

30
00:02:11,480 --> 00:02:16,440
rescued injured pigeons, was repulsed by women's jewelry,

31
00:02:16,540 --> 00:02:21,680
and claimed to receive communications from outer space.

32
00:02:21,780 --> 00:02:26,420
Our virtues and our failings are inseparable,

33
00:02:26,421 --> 00:02:28,760
like force and matter.

34
00:02:28,860 --> 00:02:32,760
When they separate, man is no more.

35
00:02:32,860 --> 00:02:33,860
Nikola Tesla.

36
00:02:38,660 --> 00:02:41,060
At the beginning of the 20th century,

37
00:02:41,160 --> 00:02:45,180
Tesla's amazing accomplishments were celebrated.

38
00:02:45,280 --> 00:02:51,820
But he died in 1943 at the New Yorker Hotel in this small room,

39
00:02:51,920 --> 00:02:55,480
virtually destitute and discredited.

40
00:02:55,481 --> 00:02:58,880
as he watched the world he helped create simply forget him.

41
00:03:02,380 --> 00:03:06,860
Nevertheless, the FBI amassed a secret file on Tesla.

42
00:03:06,960 --> 00:03:09,320
And when he died, the United States government

43
00:03:09,420 --> 00:03:12,500
took charge of his scientific papers.

44
00:03:12,600 --> 00:03:14,760
If you listen to everything he talked about,

45
00:03:14,860 --> 00:03:17,000
it was so powerful.

46
00:03:17,100 --> 00:03:19,400
It was so frightening.

47
00:03:19,500 --> 00:03:24,000
It was so terrifying that they just pounced upon all

48
00:03:24,001 --> 00:03:26,340
possession and whisked them away.

49
00:03:26,360 --> 00:03:28,980
What were the powerful and terrifying ideas

50
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:32,240
that may have threatened national security?

51
00:03:32,280 --> 00:03:35,520
Were they a madman's rantings or works of genius?

52
00:03:35,540 --> 00:03:39,120
There is still a place for Nikola Tesla's contributions.

53
00:03:39,140 --> 00:03:42,260
Many people believe that he was actually onto something.

54
00:03:42,280 --> 00:03:44,880
Maybe it's time to look back and reinvestigate

55
00:03:44,920 --> 00:03:47,520
what Tesla was really up to.

56
00:03:47,560 --> 00:03:50,100
We know one thing Tesla was up to,

57
00:03:50,120 --> 00:03:54,000
wireless energy that could be transmitted around the world.

58
00:03:54,001 --> 00:03:56,000
The wireless transmission of power

59
00:03:56,100 --> 00:03:58,100
would appear over and over again,

60
00:03:58,200 --> 00:04:00,400
even in Tesla's most exotic visions.

61
00:04:03,340 --> 00:04:06,040
The key to understanding this remarkable idea

62
00:04:06,140 --> 00:04:09,040
was this peculiar yet powerful invention,

63
00:04:09,140 --> 00:04:14,860
first patented in 1891 by Tesla at age 35,

64
00:04:14,960 --> 00:04:16,860
called the Tesla coil.

65
00:04:19,660 --> 00:04:22,060
Nothing like it had ever been done before.

66
00:04:22,061 --> 00:04:25,300
Think of the Tesla coil as a giant electrical pump.

67
00:04:25,400 --> 00:04:28,100
This is an example of a modern-day Tesla coil.

68
00:04:28,200 --> 00:04:30,340
We're going to take power from a wall outlet,

69
00:04:30,440 --> 00:04:32,300
feed it to the base of the unit, where we're

70
00:04:32,400 --> 00:04:34,380
going to step up the voltage tremendously

71
00:04:34,480 --> 00:04:36,680
until it comes out the top.

72
00:04:36,780 --> 00:04:39,340
So I'm going to step back, and we're going to take a look.

73
00:04:43,420 --> 00:04:46,760
This Tesla coil steps up standard voltage from a wall

74
00:04:46,860 --> 00:04:52,060
outlet at 120 volts to more than 500,000 volts.

75
00:04:52,061 --> 00:04:58,060
Imagine electricity flowing in a wire the same way as water

76
00:04:58,160 --> 00:05:00,060
pumped through a hose.

77
00:05:00,160 --> 00:05:03,000
The current can be likened to the flow of water.

78
00:05:03,100 --> 00:05:06,000
The voltage can be likened to water pressure.

79
00:05:06,100 --> 00:05:08,440
If a nozzle is attached to the hose,

80
00:05:08,540 --> 00:05:11,640
the force of the water pressure is dramatically increased,

81
00:05:11,740 --> 00:05:15,020
while the flow of water is decreased.

82
00:05:15,120 --> 00:05:17,620
A Tesla coil acts in the same way,

83
00:05:17,720 --> 00:05:20,660
by passing the current from a small primary coil

84
00:05:20,661 --> 00:05:23,400
to a secondary, much larger coil,

85
00:05:23,500 --> 00:05:25,740
it steps up the voltage tremendously

86
00:05:25,840 --> 00:05:28,700
and reduces the current.

87
00:05:28,800 --> 00:05:32,740
This makes the Tesla coil a transformer.

88
00:05:32,840 --> 00:05:35,780
Much like firefighters would connect a big hose

89
00:05:35,880 --> 00:05:37,980
to a fire hydrant and then use the nozzle

90
00:05:38,080 --> 00:05:40,380
to spray that water very far away,

91
00:05:40,480 --> 00:05:43,820
Tesla coil does the same thing.

92
00:05:43,920 --> 00:05:45,820
Using an enormous Tesla coil,

93
00:05:45,920 --> 00:05:48,380
the inventor wanted to pump the Earth an atmosphere

94
00:05:48,381 --> 00:05:51,420
atmosphere full of electric energy.

95
00:05:51,520 --> 00:05:52,860
He believed he could use the Earth

96
00:05:52,960 --> 00:05:55,220
as a natural conductor of electricity

97
00:05:55,320 --> 00:05:58,020
and send power around the world.

98
00:05:58,120 --> 00:06:01,220
In essence, the Earth would become a giant electrical outlet.

99
00:06:04,520 --> 00:06:06,740
To prove his wildly original theory,

100
00:06:06,840 --> 00:06:09,260
Tesla left his home in New York City

101
00:06:09,360 --> 00:06:13,080
and began experimenting with wireless power in 1899

102
00:06:13,180 --> 00:06:18,380
at the age of 43 on the windswept prairies of Colorado.

103
00:06:18,381 --> 00:06:21,580
He believed that there was some specific frequency

104
00:06:21,680 --> 00:06:26,780
at which the Earth could be struck as with a hammer.

105
00:06:26,880 --> 00:06:31,120
Tesla believed that the Earth could be rung electrically,

106
00:06:31,220 --> 00:06:35,760
just as a bell can be rung with a clapper.

107
00:06:35,860 --> 00:06:38,540
He constructed a laboratory and tower

108
00:06:38,640 --> 00:06:41,840
that soared 80 feet into the air.

109
00:06:41,940 --> 00:06:46,040
Inside the barn-like structure was an enormous Tesla coil.

110
00:06:46,041 --> 00:06:51,040
For Tesla to be creating these electrical arcs in his laboratory

111
00:06:51,140 --> 00:06:54,040
was very unsettling to the people in Colorado

112
00:06:54,140 --> 00:06:58,160
because they felt that he was conjuring the work of God.

113
00:07:02,160 --> 00:07:04,100
By tapping into the local electric power

114
00:07:04,200 --> 00:07:07,660
from Colorado Springs, Tesla's coil produced more

115
00:07:07,760 --> 00:07:10,740
than 12 million volts.

116
00:07:10,840 --> 00:07:13,160
He often demonstrated his wireless concept

117
00:07:13,161 --> 00:07:15,860
by illuminating a phosphorescent bulb

118
00:07:15,960 --> 00:07:19,200
while holding it in his hand.

119
00:07:19,300 --> 00:07:21,640
When the machine is on, the entire lab

120
00:07:21,740 --> 00:07:23,800
is filled with high-voltage electricity.

121
00:07:23,900 --> 00:07:25,540
I'm going to grab this fluorescent light

122
00:07:25,640 --> 00:07:26,440
and show you what I mean.

123
00:07:33,880 --> 00:07:36,120
The Tesla coil outputs electric energy

124
00:07:36,220 --> 00:07:39,780
without harming humans, similar to the way transmission towers

125
00:07:39,880 --> 00:07:41,380
bombard us with radio waves.

126
00:07:43,160 --> 00:07:46,400
What you're seeing is electricity fly through the air

127
00:07:46,500 --> 00:07:49,040
from the Tesla coil to the bulb lighting it.

128
00:07:49,140 --> 00:07:51,640
That's the essence of Tesla's original idea.

129
00:07:51,740 --> 00:07:55,040
He wanted to transmit power globally so that people need

130
00:07:55,140 --> 00:07:58,480
only receive it to use it.

131
00:07:58,580 --> 00:08:02,120
Tesla claimed his experiments in Colorado were a success,

132
00:08:02,220 --> 00:08:04,940
and he had achieved the wireless transmission of power,

133
00:08:05,040 --> 00:08:07,480
illuminating light bulbs up to one mile away.

134
00:08:13,160 --> 00:08:15,560
In addition to pronouncements of his Colorado electrical

135
00:08:15,660 --> 00:08:19,960
discoveries, Tesla also made an even more startling claim,

136
00:08:20,060 --> 00:08:22,500
that he received messages from outer space.

137
00:08:22,600 --> 00:08:24,700
He got a lot of flack when he came back to New York

138
00:08:24,800 --> 00:08:28,100
for saying, I've spoken to Mars, talking to Martians.

139
00:08:28,200 --> 00:08:30,640
But it's interesting, because the ideas that he

140
00:08:30,740 --> 00:08:33,640
were using are exactly the ideas that we're using now

141
00:08:33,740 --> 00:08:35,720
to send messages out into outer space.

142
00:08:35,820 --> 00:08:38,580
We're sending radio waves out into outer space to try to see

143
00:08:38,680 --> 00:08:39,880
if anyone's out there.

144
00:08:39,980 --> 00:08:42,660
So people back then really had some idea that he might be

145
00:08:42,661 --> 00:08:45,160
but he was actually probably really ahead of his time.

146
00:08:47,660 --> 00:08:50,300
Tesla believed he had crossed a threshold

147
00:08:50,400 --> 00:08:54,040
and proven he could send power wirelessly.

148
00:08:54,140 --> 00:08:56,740
He just had to find more money to build an even bigger

149
00:08:56,840 --> 00:08:57,780
transmitter.

150
00:09:04,480 --> 00:09:07,180
Tesla returned to the East Coast.

151
00:09:07,280 --> 00:09:10,080
On Long Island Sound in the town of Shoreham,

152
00:09:10,081 --> 00:09:12,920
a street sign marks the entrance to a now abandoned

153
00:09:13,020 --> 00:09:14,020
and forgotten location.

154
00:09:17,020 --> 00:09:20,620
In 1901, based on his Colorado experiments,

155
00:09:20,720 --> 00:09:23,320
Tesla began to build his visionary wireless power

156
00:09:23,420 --> 00:09:27,160
network here on this site.

157
00:09:27,260 --> 00:09:29,060
He called it Wardenclyp.

158
00:09:32,200 --> 00:09:35,580
It was comprised of a laboratory and power plant.

159
00:09:35,680 --> 00:09:40,080
Adjacent to it was a tremendous 187-foot tower.

160
00:09:40,081 --> 00:09:43,880
Power from the plant was sent to a giant Tesla coil

161
00:09:43,980 --> 00:09:45,820
in the tower.

162
00:09:45,920 --> 00:09:48,560
Underneath the tower, the inventor

163
00:09:48,660 --> 00:09:52,060
sank huge shafts 120 feet into the soil

164
00:09:52,160 --> 00:09:54,900
to transmit the electrical current into the Earth.

165
00:09:56,840 --> 00:09:59,360
This was to be the first of many transmitters

166
00:09:59,460 --> 00:10:01,660
in a system that would encircle the world

167
00:10:01,760 --> 00:10:03,640
with wireless energy.

168
00:10:03,740 --> 00:10:06,380
The vast amounts of electricity necessary

169
00:10:06,480 --> 00:10:10,080
would come from huge hydroelectric projects.

170
00:10:10,081 --> 00:10:11,640
A lot of people thought it was crazy

171
00:10:11,740 --> 00:10:13,780
because they couldn't understand what he was seeing

172
00:10:13,880 --> 00:10:15,760
and what he was envisioning for the future.

173
00:10:15,860 --> 00:10:18,560
He was able to conceive of things that other people weren't.

174
00:10:18,660 --> 00:10:21,560
He was a visionary.

175
00:10:21,660 --> 00:10:23,960
But even visionaries need money.

176
00:10:24,060 --> 00:10:27,060
So in order to convince industrialist J.P. Morgan

177
00:10:27,160 --> 00:10:31,940
to invest $150,000 to build Wardenclyp,

178
00:10:32,040 --> 00:10:34,080
Tesla promised the financier the tower

179
00:10:34,180 --> 00:10:38,580
would make millions by also broadcasting messages, news,

180
00:10:38,581 --> 00:10:41,580
music, and even pictures to any part of the world.

181
00:10:44,580 --> 00:10:48,920
This is the original foundation for Tesla's tower.

182
00:10:49,020 --> 00:10:52,820
If you look, you can see that it's an eight-sided figure.

183
00:10:52,920 --> 00:10:56,340
It had a huge hole down in the ground right in front

184
00:10:56,440 --> 00:10:59,400
of where I'm standing, 120 feet down,

185
00:10:59,500 --> 00:11:02,140
and a spiral staircase went around it.

186
00:11:02,240 --> 00:11:07,140
And then up into the sky, 187 feet was the tower.

187
00:11:07,141 --> 00:11:11,880
The tower was attached by huge timbers on bars like this

188
00:11:11,980 --> 00:11:15,380
at each of the points along the side foundation walls.

189
00:11:15,480 --> 00:11:18,280
It loomed over everything in the area.

190
00:11:18,380 --> 00:11:21,620
It was overwhelming when you think of the size and scope

191
00:11:21,720 --> 00:11:25,800
of such a huge, huge construction.

192
00:11:25,900 --> 00:11:27,900
This is the actual lab and power plant

193
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:31,400
at Wardenclyffe, where Tesla worked for more than four years,

194
00:11:31,500 --> 00:11:34,260
trying to develop his wireless concept.

195
00:11:34,360 --> 00:11:36,140
It was purchased by a photographic company

196
00:11:36,141 --> 00:11:38,540
more than 60 years ago.

197
00:11:38,640 --> 00:11:42,920
After remodeling, only a part of the outside lab remains.

198
00:11:43,020 --> 00:11:44,220
Really getting to go inside.

199
00:11:44,320 --> 00:11:45,420
This is so exciting.

200
00:11:45,520 --> 00:11:47,580
All right, this door hasn't been open in a while.

201
00:11:47,680 --> 00:11:49,180
Although the building hasn't been

202
00:11:49,280 --> 00:11:51,460
used in more than a decade, the owners

203
00:11:51,560 --> 00:11:53,360
agreed to open the doors to modern marvels

204
00:11:53,460 --> 00:11:56,500
for a rare look inside.

205
00:11:56,600 --> 00:11:58,960
Wow.

206
00:11:59,060 --> 00:12:00,460
Oh, this is incredible.

207
00:12:00,461 --> 00:12:07,460
This is amazing to be in here where Tesla worked,

208
00:12:07,461 --> 00:12:10,460
where he spent time with his assistants.

209
00:12:10,461 --> 00:12:15,460
It's just amazing that over 100 years ago,

210
00:12:15,461 --> 00:12:17,460
Tesla was walking in these very walls.

211
00:12:17,461 --> 00:12:19,460
His equipment was here.

212
00:12:19,461 --> 00:12:21,460
His dreams were alive.

213
00:12:21,461 --> 00:12:24,460
He was looking forward to what he hoped would be

214
00:12:24,461 --> 00:12:27,460
a very glorious future.

215
00:12:27,461 --> 00:12:32,460
In part, it was timing that ultimately doomed Wardenclyffe.

216
00:12:32,461 --> 00:12:35,460
On December 12, 1901, as Tesla was working

217
00:12:35,461 --> 00:12:39,460
at his wireless network, Guglielmo Marconi beat him to the punch

218
00:12:39,461 --> 00:12:44,460
and successfully transmitted a radio signal across the Atlantic.

219
00:12:44,461 --> 00:12:47,460
While Marconi's achievement was indeed a first,

220
00:12:47,461 --> 00:12:50,460
in reality, he used 17 of Tesla's patents

221
00:12:50,461 --> 00:12:52,460
to accomplish this feat.

222
00:12:52,461 --> 00:12:56,460
Tesla was not only forgotten as the father of radio.

223
00:12:56,461 --> 00:13:01,460
Marconi's transmission sealed the fate of Wardenclyffe.

224
00:13:01,560 --> 00:13:03,260
Morgan was no longer interested

225
00:13:03,360 --> 00:13:05,600
in supporting Tesla's work here.

226
00:13:05,700 --> 00:13:06,900
Marconi already did it.

227
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:08,900
Why should he keep supporting Tesla?

228
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:12,540
And of course, Tesla's plan was greater than Morgan knew.

229
00:13:12,640 --> 00:13:15,020
Tesla could see reason to continue the funding

230
00:13:15,120 --> 00:13:19,820
and continue the work, but it wasn't Morgan's plan.

231
00:13:19,920 --> 00:13:22,920
So in 1905, while still under construction

232
00:13:23,020 --> 00:13:25,920
and after some amazing electrical displays,

233
00:13:25,921 --> 00:13:30,400
the Wardenclyffe project was abandoned and later destroyed.

234
00:13:33,700 --> 00:13:35,900
The world was not prepared for it.

235
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:38,000
It was too far ahead of its time.

236
00:13:38,100 --> 00:13:40,400
But the same laws will prevail in the end

237
00:13:40,500 --> 00:13:43,140
and make it a triumphal success.

238
00:13:43,240 --> 00:13:46,540
Nikola Tesla.

239
00:13:46,640 --> 00:13:49,640
But could Tesla have succeeded?

240
00:13:49,740 --> 00:13:52,640
The mainstream scientific community has grave doubts.

241
00:13:52,641 --> 00:13:57,640
Tesla's schemes for worldwide electrification

242
00:13:57,740 --> 00:13:59,920
was simply out of the question.

243
00:14:00,020 --> 00:14:03,280
It makes no sense from the standpoint of the energy

244
00:14:03,380 --> 00:14:06,880
involved and the inefficiency of it.

245
00:14:06,980 --> 00:14:09,360
Still, some aren't sure.

246
00:14:09,460 --> 00:14:11,920
I believe that the wireless transmission of power

247
00:14:12,020 --> 00:14:13,200
is possible.

248
00:14:13,300 --> 00:14:16,960
The chance that power could be transmitted around the globe

249
00:14:17,060 --> 00:14:22,640
without wires was too great a discovery to pass up.

250
00:14:22,641 --> 00:14:25,040
Jeff Parisi, a high-voltage technician

251
00:14:25,041 --> 00:14:28,320
from KVA Effects in Signal Hill, California,

252
00:14:28,321 --> 00:14:30,960
was part of a team that built the largest Tesla coil

253
00:14:30,961 --> 00:14:35,020
in modern times, the 13M.

254
00:14:39,700 --> 00:14:42,660
The reason the 13M was built was to give scientists

255
00:14:42,661 --> 00:14:45,740
the opportunity to actually investigate some of Tesla's

256
00:14:45,741 --> 00:14:50,080
wireless transmission theories to see if they're practical.

257
00:14:50,081 --> 00:14:55,280
The 13M generates close to 12 million volts of power.

258
00:14:55,281 --> 00:14:58,680
In 1998, the team tested the giant Tesla coil.

259
00:15:03,060 --> 00:15:04,820
What we learned is that the machine we built

260
00:15:04,821 --> 00:15:06,600
is not large enough.

261
00:15:06,601 --> 00:15:09,460
It's actually a quarter scale version of what Tesla made

262
00:15:09,461 --> 00:15:10,900
in Colorado Springs.

263
00:15:10,901 --> 00:15:13,940
And it seems a machine that large is really necessary.

264
00:15:13,941 --> 00:15:16,480
We hope in the future that we'll have the funding

265
00:15:16,481 --> 00:15:19,540
to be able to build a full scale Tesla coil

266
00:15:19,541 --> 00:15:22,340
and prove his theories.

267
00:15:22,341 --> 00:15:24,640
How can it be that a century after the failure

268
00:15:24,641 --> 00:15:27,540
of Wardenclyffe, Tesla's ideas are still

269
00:15:27,541 --> 00:15:30,380
so passionately considered?

270
00:15:30,381 --> 00:15:33,880
Perhaps it's because long before Wardenclyffe,

271
00:15:33,881 --> 00:15:35,780
Tesla had already changed the world.

272
00:15:40,900 --> 00:15:45,800
On June 21, 1943, the United States Supreme Court reversed

273
00:15:45,801 --> 00:15:49,300
granting patent rights to Tesla, not Marconi,

274
00:15:49,400 --> 00:15:51,440
for the invention of radio.

275
00:15:51,540 --> 00:15:54,840
Mad Electricity will return on Modern Marvels.

276
00:15:59,140 --> 00:16:02,860
We now return to Mad Electricity on Modern Marvels.

277
00:16:07,960 --> 00:16:11,860
Today, we take our electrified world for granted,

278
00:16:11,960 --> 00:16:14,600
energy that powers our needs.

279
00:16:14,601 --> 00:16:17,100
is as close as the nearest outlet.

280
00:16:19,300 --> 00:16:21,880
According to legend, the man who made it happen

281
00:16:21,980 --> 00:16:26,680
was born in Croatia in 1856 at the stroke of midnight

282
00:16:26,780 --> 00:16:28,180
during an electrical storm.

283
00:16:30,420 --> 00:16:33,360
Tesla's immediately associated with thunder and lightning

284
00:16:33,460 --> 00:16:34,360
with electricity.

285
00:16:41,060 --> 00:16:43,800
Tesla began his career as an electrical engineer

286
00:16:43,801 --> 00:16:48,500
with a telephone company in Budapest in 1881.

287
00:16:48,600 --> 00:16:51,500
One day, as he was walking through a park with a friend,

288
00:16:51,600 --> 00:16:54,280
Tesla suddenly envisioned a groundbreaking concept

289
00:16:54,380 --> 00:16:57,540
for a new electric motor and drew it out in the dirt.

290
00:17:00,380 --> 00:17:02,860
This simple illustration became the patent

291
00:17:02,960 --> 00:17:06,160
for the induction motor, which would go on to be the standard

292
00:17:06,260 --> 00:17:07,660
electric motor for the world.

293
00:17:10,400 --> 00:17:13,300
It's used for everything, from tools and appliances,

294
00:17:13,301 --> 00:17:18,300
to hybrid appliances, to hybrid cars, to industrial plants.

295
00:17:21,300 --> 00:17:24,300
The induction motor works by energizing coils of wire

296
00:17:24,301 --> 00:17:29,300
placed around a stationary frame called a stator.

297
00:17:29,301 --> 00:17:33,300
This induces the current in the coils onto a rotor.

298
00:17:33,301 --> 00:17:35,300
The alternating current in the coils

299
00:17:35,301 --> 00:17:37,300
pauses the poles of the magnetic fields around them

300
00:17:37,301 --> 00:17:41,300
to change between north and south.

301
00:17:41,301 --> 00:17:43,940
The resulting attraction and repulsion with the coils

302
00:17:44,040 --> 00:17:46,240
as they alternate causes the rotor to spin.

303
00:17:50,740 --> 00:17:53,540
And the exact same ideas that Nicola Tesla came up

304
00:17:53,640 --> 00:17:57,780
with 100 years ago are still used today in induction motors

305
00:17:57,880 --> 00:17:59,080
such as this one.

306
00:17:59,180 --> 00:18:05,420
The motor is made of two parts, the rotor and the coil.

307
00:18:05,520 --> 00:18:09,220
Within the coil, a north-south magnetic field rotates.

308
00:18:09,221 --> 00:18:12,560
And as a magnetic field rotates, the rotor follows it.

309
00:18:15,220 --> 00:18:17,620
Just like it did 100 years ago when Nikola Tesla

310
00:18:17,720 --> 00:18:18,860
made the first prototype.

311
00:18:22,900 --> 00:18:26,880
In 1884, at age 28, Tesla moved to America

312
00:18:26,980 --> 00:18:29,600
with little money and only a letter of recommendation

313
00:18:29,700 --> 00:18:33,280
from his boss to Thomas Edison.

314
00:18:33,380 --> 00:18:36,840
The letter simply read, I know two great men,

315
00:18:36,841 --> 00:18:39,640
men, and you are one of them.

316
00:18:39,740 --> 00:18:43,340
This young man is the other.

317
00:18:43,440 --> 00:18:45,640
Edison hired the brilliant young engineer

318
00:18:45,740 --> 00:18:47,880
and eventually asked him to redesign his company's

319
00:18:47,980 --> 00:18:53,220
electric generators for a $50,000 bonus.

320
00:18:53,320 --> 00:18:55,360
After Tesla developed a number of enormously

321
00:18:55,460 --> 00:19:00,860
profitable new patents, he asked Edison for his bonus.

322
00:19:00,960 --> 00:19:03,760
Edison says, you've got to be kidding me.

323
00:19:03,860 --> 00:19:06,840
You've got a lot to learn about an American sense of humor.

324
00:19:06,841 --> 00:19:09,240
And he doesn't give him the $50,000.

325
00:19:09,340 --> 00:19:11,440
And Tesla turns around immediately and says,

326
00:19:11,540 --> 00:19:14,980
well, Mr. Edison, I resign.

327
00:19:15,080 --> 00:19:18,980
So began a lifelong feud between the upstart young genius

328
00:19:19,080 --> 00:19:22,180
and the established inventor.

329
00:19:22,280 --> 00:19:25,700
Tesla leaves Edison's, and he was digging ditches

330
00:19:25,800 --> 00:19:28,800
in New York City for a year, a very, very dark time.

331
00:19:28,900 --> 00:19:33,440
All of his dreams are in shambles at this point.

332
00:19:33,540 --> 00:19:35,800
While toiling to feed himself and raise capital

333
00:19:35,801 --> 00:19:37,560
for new inventions.

334
00:19:37,660 --> 00:19:39,900
Tesla was appalled at the web of power wires

335
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:40,800
strung up in the city.

336
00:19:43,440 --> 00:19:46,240
The whole system was a nightmare of cables

337
00:19:46,340 --> 00:19:47,840
and wiring overhead.

338
00:19:47,940 --> 00:19:52,040
Matter of fact, in some areas, it even blocked out the sun.

339
00:19:52,140 --> 00:19:54,460
The electrical system he found deficient

340
00:19:54,560 --> 00:19:58,560
was called direct current, or DC.

341
00:19:58,660 --> 00:20:02,200
Edison, Tesla's former employer, was a major investor in DC

342
00:20:02,300 --> 00:20:05,000
power.

343
00:20:05,001 --> 00:20:07,160
Tesla knew there was a better way

344
00:20:07,260 --> 00:20:09,840
and was determined to invent a new system

345
00:20:09,940 --> 00:20:12,840
that will become the global standard,

346
00:20:12,940 --> 00:20:17,140
alternating current, or AC.

347
00:20:17,240 --> 00:20:20,240
Here's a direct current cable that would carry

348
00:20:20,340 --> 00:20:23,820
about a million watts to light a typical New York City block.

349
00:20:23,920 --> 00:20:26,980
Using alternating current in Tesla's AC system,

350
00:20:27,080 --> 00:20:29,320
a small wire such as this could power

351
00:20:29,420 --> 00:20:31,320
the same amount of homes.

352
00:20:31,420 --> 00:20:35,000
Same amount of power, just look at the difference.

353
00:20:35,001 --> 00:20:37,900
The difference between AC and DC power

354
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:40,700
is all about how electricity, or electrons, flow.

355
00:20:44,640 --> 00:20:47,940
For DC current to work, there must be a continuous and direct

356
00:20:48,040 --> 00:20:53,660
flow of electrons along a wire, from negative to positive poles.

357
00:20:53,760 --> 00:20:56,960
When power is applied, electrons, as signified

358
00:20:57,060 --> 00:21:00,860
by the red band, move to the work, do the work,

359
00:21:00,960 --> 00:21:03,760
and then come all the way back to the generator.

360
00:21:03,761 --> 00:21:07,060
The problem with this process is that the electrons

361
00:21:07,061 --> 00:21:10,160
encounter resistance along these wires.

362
00:21:10,360 --> 00:21:13,520
It's difficult for electrons to travel these great distances.

363
00:21:14,100 --> 00:21:17,640
So most of the energy in this system is lost in the wire.

364
00:21:18,280 --> 00:21:20,700
Well, the way Thomas Edison envisioned his system,

365
00:21:20,701 --> 00:21:23,840
he would have to put a power plant just about every mile

366
00:21:23,841 --> 00:21:27,660
to keep the voltage steady along his DC power grid.

367
00:21:28,540 --> 00:21:31,960
In 1887, Tesla filed seven U.S. patents

368
00:21:31,961 --> 00:21:35,260
for another more efficient and cheaper power system

369
00:21:35,360 --> 00:21:36,960
called alternating current.

370
00:21:40,460 --> 00:21:43,200
What Tesla discovered in his AC system

371
00:21:43,300 --> 00:21:46,640
was it was not necessary to send the electrons all the way

372
00:21:46,740 --> 00:21:48,240
to the work and back again.

373
00:21:48,340 --> 00:21:52,180
In fact, he alternated the current,

374
00:21:52,280 --> 00:21:54,880
sending it back and forth.

375
00:21:54,980 --> 00:21:58,120
Tesla developed a system of AC generators

376
00:21:58,220 --> 00:22:00,560
that alternated electric current between negative

377
00:22:00,561 --> 00:22:05,060
positive poles at 60 cycles per second.

378
00:22:05,160 --> 00:22:06,860
By sending AC through a transformer

379
00:22:06,960 --> 00:22:10,600
with virtually no power loss, he could step up the voltage

380
00:22:10,700 --> 00:22:13,740
and lower the current so that AC could be efficiently

381
00:22:13,840 --> 00:22:16,480
transmitted hundreds of miles further than DC.

382
00:22:19,440 --> 00:22:22,040
Millionaire entrepreneur George Westinghouse

383
00:22:22,140 --> 00:22:25,320
thought Tesla's inventions could be the key to long-distance

384
00:22:25,420 --> 00:22:27,680
power transmission.

385
00:22:27,681 --> 00:22:31,580
He purchased the patents for $60,000

386
00:22:31,680 --> 00:22:35,620
and a healthy share of stock in the Westinghouse Corporation.

387
00:22:35,720 --> 00:22:38,220
If the new AC system was successful,

388
00:22:38,320 --> 00:22:39,620
Tesla would be a rich man.

389
00:22:42,420 --> 00:22:48,240
Nikola Tesla became a citizen of the United States in 1891.

390
00:22:48,340 --> 00:22:51,040
The same year, an all-out current war

391
00:22:51,140 --> 00:22:56,140
erupted between his AC and Edison's DC power.

392
00:22:56,141 --> 00:22:58,540
Edison launched a propaganda campaign

393
00:22:58,640 --> 00:23:01,540
to show the dangers of AC.

394
00:23:01,640 --> 00:23:05,120
The public, they had rather grotesque experiments

395
00:23:05,220 --> 00:23:07,880
where they would take animals and electrocute them

396
00:23:07,980 --> 00:23:09,160
with alternating current.

397
00:23:13,520 --> 00:23:15,160
Edison convinced New York State

398
00:23:15,260 --> 00:23:18,020
to use Tesla's and Westinghouse's AC power

399
00:23:18,120 --> 00:23:22,460
for the very first electrocution in 1890.

400
00:23:22,461 --> 00:23:25,260
A reporter called it a gruesome spectacle,

401
00:23:25,360 --> 00:23:26,700
far worse than hanging.

402
00:23:29,400 --> 00:23:33,000
Edison dubbed the technique Westinghousing.

403
00:23:33,100 --> 00:23:35,200
Whether it was electrocuting animals

404
00:23:35,300 --> 00:23:38,540
or electrocuting prisoners, the thrust was,

405
00:23:38,640 --> 00:23:41,980
this is something you don't want in your house.

406
00:23:42,080 --> 00:23:44,880
In 1893, despite the bad press,

407
00:23:44,980 --> 00:23:46,880
Tesla and the Westinghouse Corporation

408
00:23:46,980 --> 00:23:50,620
won the bid for illuminating the Chicago World's Fair,

409
00:23:50,621 --> 00:23:54,620
The first all-electric fare in history.

410
00:23:54,720 --> 00:23:57,020
Edison, who made an unsuccessful bid,

411
00:23:57,120 --> 00:24:00,260
was frustrated over losing this opportunity

412
00:24:00,360 --> 00:24:03,360
and refused to let Tesla use his patented light bulbs.

413
00:24:06,860 --> 00:24:10,580
So Tesla needed to come up with a new light bulb,

414
00:24:10,680 --> 00:24:14,480
make 250,000 of them in six months' time to light the fare.

415
00:24:14,580 --> 00:24:17,180
Edison's bulb had a screw base at the bottom.

416
00:24:17,280 --> 00:24:19,580
He patented the whole method of powering the bulb

417
00:24:19,581 --> 00:24:23,420
through this screw base and how to seal the vacuum off inside.

418
00:24:23,520 --> 00:24:26,160
Tesla's solution was to have a ground glass stopper

419
00:24:26,260 --> 00:24:27,520
in the bottom of the bulb.

420
00:24:27,620 --> 00:24:29,420
The wires passed through the stopper,

421
00:24:29,520 --> 00:24:32,860
and he was able to manufacture a bulb that didn't interfere

422
00:24:32,960 --> 00:24:34,300
with Edison's patents.

423
00:24:34,400 --> 00:24:36,600
Tesla was able to beat Edison in his own game

424
00:24:36,700 --> 00:24:39,460
by producing a lamp that was more easy to manufacture,

425
00:24:39,560 --> 00:24:44,000
and he was able to do so in a short amount of time.

426
00:24:44,100 --> 00:24:47,400
On May 1, 1893, President Grover

427
00:24:47,401 --> 00:24:49,900
President Grover Cleveland pushed a button,

428
00:24:50,000 --> 00:24:53,740
and more than 200,000 of Tesla's incandescent lamps

429
00:24:53,840 --> 00:24:56,900
illuminated the fairgrounds.

430
00:24:57,000 --> 00:25:00,640
It was a monumental success and ushered in the era

431
00:25:00,740 --> 00:25:03,340
of modern electric lighting.

432
00:25:03,440 --> 00:25:05,760
Tesla was held the mastermind and the genius

433
00:25:05,860 --> 00:25:07,560
behind making this possible.

434
00:25:07,660 --> 00:25:12,500
And his name was known around the world after this event.

435
00:25:12,600 --> 00:25:14,960
Tesla, filled with confidence after this victory

436
00:25:14,961 --> 00:25:17,460
believed AC would be the current of the future.

437
00:25:21,460 --> 00:25:23,260
In order to prove it, he would try

438
00:25:23,360 --> 00:25:26,700
to harness the power of one of the world's greatest natural

439
00:25:26,800 --> 00:25:27,500
wonders.

440
00:25:30,300 --> 00:25:34,420
In 1898, Tesla invented the electrical igniter

441
00:25:34,520 --> 00:25:36,220
for gas engines.

442
00:25:36,320 --> 00:25:40,020
Today, it's used in more than 600 million cars worldwide

443
00:25:40,120 --> 00:25:44,960
and is better known as the automobile ignition system.

444
00:25:44,961 --> 00:25:47,960
Mad Electricity will return on Modern Marvels.

445
00:25:52,400 --> 00:25:55,900
We now return to Mad Electricity on Modern Marvels.

446
00:26:00,000 --> 00:26:03,680
Millions have witnessed its stunning power.

447
00:26:03,780 --> 00:26:07,780
Niagara Falls can send more than 750,000 gallons of water

448
00:26:07,880 --> 00:26:12,880
over its crest every second, producing enough power

449
00:26:12,881 --> 00:26:17,480
power to generate 2.4 million kilowatts of electricity.

450
00:26:17,580 --> 00:26:20,520
At that rate, the falls could power half of Las Vegas

451
00:26:20,620 --> 00:26:21,720
on a hot summer night.

452
00:26:25,720 --> 00:26:28,220
Ever since pioneers began building sawmills

453
00:26:28,320 --> 00:26:31,240
along the Niagara River in the 1700s,

454
00:26:31,340 --> 00:26:34,240
many had envisioned grand new ways to harness the thunderous

455
00:26:34,340 --> 00:26:35,240
power of the falls.

456
00:26:35,241 --> 00:26:43,240
In 1893, after witnessing Tesla and Westinghouse's triumph

457
00:26:43,340 --> 00:26:47,220
at the Chicago World's Fair, the Niagara Falls Commission

458
00:26:47,320 --> 00:26:49,260
awarded them the contract to tap the falls

459
00:26:49,360 --> 00:26:52,920
for generating alternating current.

460
00:26:53,020 --> 00:26:55,720
The big problem with using Niagara Falls

461
00:26:55,820 --> 00:26:59,560
for electricity was that the major consumer

462
00:26:59,660 --> 00:27:02,860
of that electric power was Buffalo.

463
00:27:02,861 --> 00:27:09,160
Buffalo is about 20 miles or so, and using DC transmission

464
00:27:09,260 --> 00:27:12,940
was totally impractical because a repeater station

465
00:27:13,040 --> 00:27:16,340
would have to have been built every two miles.

466
00:27:16,440 --> 00:27:20,080
AC avoided all of that.

467
00:27:20,180 --> 00:27:22,820
At the falls, Tesla designed and engineered

468
00:27:22,920 --> 00:27:25,520
a complex system of generators and transformers

469
00:27:25,620 --> 00:27:27,280
to produce his AC power.

470
00:27:27,281 --> 00:27:29,820
Tesla didn't write down everything.

471
00:27:29,920 --> 00:27:31,620
Most of his stuff was in his head.

472
00:27:31,720 --> 00:27:33,220
He had to do it verbally.

473
00:27:33,320 --> 00:27:35,380
Tesla was in his own little world, more or less.

474
00:27:35,480 --> 00:27:37,920
He would not explain things to other people,

475
00:27:38,020 --> 00:27:40,520
but his assistants would.

476
00:27:40,620 --> 00:27:44,260
In 1996, the first AC hydroelectric station

477
00:27:44,360 --> 00:27:48,760
for long-distance power transmission went online,

478
00:27:48,860 --> 00:27:53,180
and Buffalo became the premier city for AC power's potential.

479
00:27:53,181 --> 00:27:58,180
the premier city for AC power's potential.

480
00:27:58,280 --> 00:28:00,780
Tesla was not here when they actually flipped the switch,

481
00:28:00,880 --> 00:28:03,120
but he had so much confidence in his own work

482
00:28:03,220 --> 00:28:06,320
that he knew it would work.

483
00:28:06,420 --> 00:28:08,520
Today, new power plants have replaced

484
00:28:08,620 --> 00:28:11,340
Tesla's structures.

485
00:28:11,440 --> 00:28:14,040
The buildings of one of the most important historical sites

486
00:28:14,140 --> 00:28:18,980
in the modern world now stand like dinosaurs, forgotten.

487
00:28:19,080 --> 00:28:21,080
And for the man who envisioned it all,

488
00:28:21,081 --> 00:28:26,580
Only an isolated statue marks his extraordinary achievement.

489
00:28:26,680 --> 00:28:33,260
I wonder how many people who come by here, past this statue,

490
00:28:33,360 --> 00:28:38,300
actually have any idea who Nikola Tesla was

491
00:28:38,400 --> 00:28:40,940
and what he did to shape their world.

492
00:28:43,640 --> 00:28:45,880
Today, our world is ablaze thanks

493
00:28:45,980 --> 00:28:48,740
to Tesla's AC technology.

494
00:28:48,741 --> 00:28:51,380
He had won his final battle with Edison,

495
00:28:51,480 --> 00:28:54,580
but victory came at an enormous cost.

496
00:28:54,680 --> 00:28:56,440
Westinghouse was financially drained

497
00:28:56,540 --> 00:28:58,420
by the war of the currents.

498
00:28:58,520 --> 00:29:01,360
In a grand gesture to keep the company afloat,

499
00:29:01,460 --> 00:29:03,960
Tesla tore up his royalties contract,

500
00:29:04,060 --> 00:29:06,660
claiming better inventions lay ahead.

501
00:29:06,760 --> 00:29:08,860
Had the contract been fulfilled,

502
00:29:08,960 --> 00:29:11,600
Tesla would have been a millionaire

503
00:29:11,700 --> 00:29:16,300
and been free of financial worries the rest of his life.

504
00:29:16,301 --> 00:29:18,800
Money does not represent such a value

505
00:29:18,900 --> 00:29:20,640
as men have placed upon it.

506
00:29:20,740 --> 00:29:23,700
All my money has been invested into experiments

507
00:29:23,800 --> 00:29:26,140
with which I have made new discoveries,

508
00:29:26,240 --> 00:29:31,640
enabling mankind to have a little easier life.

509
00:29:31,740 --> 00:29:33,520
After the triumph over Edison

510
00:29:33,620 --> 00:29:35,960
and the success of his alternating current,

511
00:29:36,060 --> 00:29:39,760
Nikola Tesla was a celebrity.

512
00:29:39,860 --> 00:29:41,800
And he's friends with Mark Twain.

513
00:29:41,900 --> 00:29:44,100
He's friends with all manner of ambassadors

514
00:29:44,101 --> 00:29:46,240
and ballerinas and prima donnas.

515
00:29:46,340 --> 00:29:49,840
And he's really kind of at the height of society.

516
00:29:49,940 --> 00:29:52,900
Genius often comes with a price.

517
00:29:53,000 --> 00:29:55,880
Tesla suffered from bizarre compulsions,

518
00:29:55,980 --> 00:29:58,820
like his consuming need to rescue injured pigeons

519
00:29:58,920 --> 00:30:00,680
and nurse them back to health.

520
00:30:00,780 --> 00:30:03,380
He has an infirmary for them right outside his bedroom

521
00:30:03,480 --> 00:30:05,620
window where he's trying to heal some that

522
00:30:05,720 --> 00:30:07,020
have broken wings, broken legs.

523
00:30:07,120 --> 00:30:09,100
And in fact, at one point, he actually

524
00:30:09,200 --> 00:30:11,960
referred to one bird as his wife.

525
00:30:11,961 --> 00:30:14,760
And he said, you know, when she died,

526
00:30:14,860 --> 00:30:17,940
the inventive spirit left him.

527
00:30:18,040 --> 00:30:20,760
Tesla had a fear of germs, compelled him

528
00:30:20,860 --> 00:30:23,100
to wash his hands constantly.

529
00:30:23,200 --> 00:30:26,140
He did things in threes and was adamant about living

530
00:30:26,240 --> 00:30:29,340
at the New Yorker Hotel in a room with a number divisible

531
00:30:29,440 --> 00:30:31,180
by three.

532
00:30:31,280 --> 00:30:35,620
He demanded the room 3,327, where he lived for 10 years.

533
00:30:35,720 --> 00:30:39,020
He always requested nine napkins.

534
00:30:39,120 --> 00:30:40,660
And if he had a bread or something,

535
00:30:40,661 --> 00:30:44,160
it could be cut into nine, three times three pieces.

536
00:30:44,260 --> 00:30:47,440
Even the number of dishes he had or the number of towels

537
00:30:47,540 --> 00:30:51,240
he received in his room was usually three or nine.

538
00:30:51,340 --> 00:30:54,900
Tesla never married and claimed he was celibate.

539
00:30:55,000 --> 00:30:57,780
He had a horrible affliction against women

540
00:30:57,880 --> 00:30:58,880
with pierced ears.

541
00:30:58,980 --> 00:31:01,620
He couldn't stand the sight of pierced ears and jewelry.

542
00:31:01,720 --> 00:31:04,780
And pearl earrings, he said, just set his teeth on edge.

543
00:31:04,880 --> 00:31:06,860
It was almost like fingers on a chalkboard for him

544
00:31:06,960 --> 00:31:09,720
to see pearl earrings.

545
00:31:09,721 --> 00:31:12,720
Even with his list of obsessions and compulsions,

546
00:31:12,820 --> 00:31:15,460
Tesla had always been able to tap into his remarkable,

547
00:31:15,560 --> 00:31:18,120
inventive intellect.

548
00:31:18,220 --> 00:31:22,160
In 1898, he gave birth to remote control

549
00:31:22,260 --> 00:31:25,160
when he demonstrated a six-foot-long radio wave

550
00:31:25,260 --> 00:31:28,180
controlled boat at Madison Square Garden.

551
00:31:35,820 --> 00:31:38,720
On a quiet lake in Riverside, California,

552
00:31:38,721 --> 00:31:42,520
Tesla's work is alive and well as a fleet of remote-controlled

553
00:31:42,620 --> 00:31:44,060
model boats set sail.

554
00:31:47,320 --> 00:31:53,460
This is very much like Tesla did 100 years ago.

555
00:31:53,560 --> 00:31:56,000
Inside these boats are the basic components

556
00:31:56,100 --> 00:31:58,480
of remote control Tesla conceived at the turn

557
00:31:58,580 --> 00:31:59,580
of the 20th century.

558
00:32:04,020 --> 00:32:06,940
A battery inside the remote control device sends power

559
00:32:06,941 --> 00:32:09,700
sends power to an oscillator that converts it

560
00:32:09,701 --> 00:32:12,680
into a radio wave pulse.

561
00:32:12,681 --> 00:32:15,080
The radio wave is transmitted to a small receiver

562
00:32:15,081 --> 00:32:19,780
on the boat, which sends the electric pulse to a motor.

563
00:32:19,781 --> 00:32:21,420
Varying the pulse of the radio wave

564
00:32:21,421 --> 00:32:23,380
from the transmitter to the receiver

565
00:32:23,381 --> 00:32:26,200
causes the motor's arms to move in different directions.

566
00:32:28,920 --> 00:32:33,300
Using a joystick, the operator controls the radio pulse signal

567
00:32:33,301 --> 00:32:34,440
and controls the boat.

568
00:32:36,940 --> 00:32:40,080
Tesla's remote control principles are seen today

569
00:32:40,180 --> 00:32:45,580
in everything from televisions to unmanned military predator

570
00:32:45,680 --> 00:32:49,680
drones used for battlefield reconnaissance

571
00:32:49,780 --> 00:32:53,020
to satellites operating in space.

572
00:32:53,120 --> 00:32:55,020
Although many of Tesla's inventions

573
00:32:55,120 --> 00:32:58,460
would prove to be a benefit to society,

574
00:32:58,560 --> 00:33:04,440
his visionary thoughts also traveled down a darker road

575
00:33:04,441 --> 00:33:08,440
to more frightening and destructive destinations.

576
00:33:12,440 --> 00:33:17,440
In 1917, Tesla proposed the concept of radio waves

577
00:33:17,441 --> 00:33:21,440
reflecting off objects to determine position and speed.

578
00:33:21,441 --> 00:33:26,440
That was 17 years before the invention of radar.

579
00:33:26,441 --> 00:33:29,440
Mad Electricity will return on Modern Marvels,

580
00:33:29,441 --> 00:33:31,440
here on History.

581
00:33:35,440 --> 00:33:38,040
We now return to Mad Electricity

582
00:33:38,140 --> 00:33:39,440
on Modern Marvels.

583
00:33:43,840 --> 00:33:46,480
One of the most powerful forces in nature

584
00:33:46,580 --> 00:33:48,580
is an earthquake.

585
00:33:48,680 --> 00:33:51,200
Suppose that power was harnessed as a weapon.

586
00:33:55,000 --> 00:33:58,840
Some believe Tesla may have discovered a way.

587
00:33:58,940 --> 00:34:01,600
The Tesla earthquake machine was a mechanical oscillator,

588
00:34:01,700 --> 00:34:03,560
which is basically a machine that produces

589
00:34:03,561 --> 00:34:05,500
that produces vibrations.

590
00:34:05,600 --> 00:34:07,560
And by producing these vibrations,

591
00:34:07,660 --> 00:34:09,900
the machine could be made to resonate

592
00:34:10,000 --> 00:34:12,400
with different structures.

593
00:34:12,500 --> 00:34:15,240
The oscillator machine is based on the principle

594
00:34:15,340 --> 00:34:17,740
that every substance, when stimulated,

595
00:34:17,840 --> 00:34:19,580
has a resonant frequency.

596
00:34:19,680 --> 00:34:21,640
If the frequency is matched and amplified

597
00:34:21,740 --> 00:34:25,280
by an outside force, such as the oscillator machine,

598
00:34:25,380 --> 00:34:30,260
any material can be literally shaken to pieces.

599
00:34:30,360 --> 00:34:33,120
The destruction of the Tacoma Narrows Bridge in Washington

600
00:34:33,121 --> 00:34:36,820
in 1940 demonstrated this principle.

601
00:34:36,920 --> 00:34:38,420
The winds whipping around the bridge

602
00:34:38,520 --> 00:34:41,620
hit just the right frequency, causing the steel bridge

603
00:34:41,720 --> 00:34:44,700
to sway violently in a rhythmic pattern that

604
00:34:44,800 --> 00:34:45,840
finally broke it apart.

605
00:34:50,200 --> 00:34:51,900
To demonstrate the power of resonance,

606
00:34:52,000 --> 00:34:55,080
we're going to see what happens to a wine glass as it vibrates

607
00:34:55,180 --> 00:34:56,640
to its breaking point.

608
00:34:56,740 --> 00:35:01,520
First, I'll start the glass vibrating and use a microphone

609
00:35:01,521 --> 00:35:04,520
connected to an oscilloscope to see what frequency the glass

610
00:35:04,521 --> 00:35:06,260
vibrates at.

611
00:35:06,261 --> 00:35:11,960
I'll then set that frequency on a signal generator,

612
00:35:11,961 --> 00:35:14,460
which is going to play back through an amplifier

613
00:35:14,461 --> 00:35:15,960
into a speaker.

614
00:35:15,961 --> 00:35:18,760
Because the glass is going to be moving more than 400 times

615
00:35:18,761 --> 00:35:21,680
per second, we will be using a strobe light in order

616
00:35:21,681 --> 00:35:23,780
to slow down the motion.

617
00:35:23,781 --> 00:35:28,080
Now, this is going to be loud, so I have to wear some protection.

618
00:35:28,081 --> 00:35:30,380
As the vibrations increase in intensity,

619
00:35:30,381 --> 00:35:32,620
the glass will start to move more and more.

620
00:35:32,720 --> 00:35:35,120
And eventually, it can't take it anymore.

621
00:35:35,220 --> 00:35:37,820
So as I turn up the volume, the glass will break.

622
00:36:00,380 --> 00:36:03,880
So what we saw was like a mini earthquake,

623
00:36:03,980 --> 00:36:06,080
and Tesla's earthquake machine would

624
00:36:06,180 --> 00:36:10,220
be able to do this, but on a much larger scale.

625
00:36:10,320 --> 00:36:13,900
In 1898, Tesla claimed to have created an oscillator

626
00:36:14,000 --> 00:36:15,860
with an adjustable frequency that

627
00:36:15,960 --> 00:36:18,640
was no bigger than an alarm clock,

628
00:36:18,740 --> 00:36:20,800
and wrote of an attempt to use it on a building

629
00:36:20,900 --> 00:36:21,740
under construction.

630
00:36:23,940 --> 00:36:27,880
In a few minutes, I felt the building beginning to tremble.

631
00:36:27,881 --> 00:36:31,480
10 minutes more, and I could have laid it in the street.

632
00:36:31,580 --> 00:36:34,520
And with the same device, I could have dropped the Brooklyn

633
00:36:34,620 --> 00:36:37,960
Bridge into the East River in less than an hour.

634
00:36:40,500 --> 00:36:42,420
Perhaps this opened a Pandora's box

635
00:36:42,520 --> 00:36:46,300
during later experiments with the device.

636
00:36:46,400 --> 00:36:51,240
There's a legend that he started up his little oscillator,

637
00:36:51,340 --> 00:36:53,480
or what some call the earthquake machine,

638
00:36:53,580 --> 00:36:55,940
and he started vibrating lower Manhattan.

639
00:36:55,941 --> 00:36:58,980
and they called went out to the police department.

640
00:36:59,080 --> 00:37:00,420
And the police came running.

641
00:37:00,520 --> 00:37:01,820
They were pounding on the door.

642
00:37:01,920 --> 00:37:04,220
They broke into his lab, and they saw him

643
00:37:04,320 --> 00:37:05,880
with a sledgehammer smashing the machine.

644
00:37:05,980 --> 00:37:06,960
The switch got stuck.

645
00:37:07,060 --> 00:37:08,020
He couldn't turn it off.

646
00:37:08,120 --> 00:37:11,720
And he almost brought down some buildings in the neighborhood.

647
00:37:11,820 --> 00:37:17,060
In 1934, Tesla conceived a death ray.

648
00:37:17,160 --> 00:37:20,740
His idea was to blast concentrated beams of particles

649
00:37:20,840 --> 00:37:23,300
charged with millions of volts of electricity

650
00:37:23,301 --> 00:37:27,100
through the air, which could down fleets of enemy aircraft

651
00:37:27,200 --> 00:37:30,140
at a distance of 250 miles.

652
00:37:33,240 --> 00:37:36,480
Tesla terrified people with this idea of the death ray.

653
00:37:36,580 --> 00:37:38,520
I mean, even the name death ray, it sounds like it's

654
00:37:38,620 --> 00:37:40,660
science fiction, right?

655
00:37:40,760 --> 00:37:43,780
Tesla spent his later years in two small rooms

656
00:37:43,880 --> 00:37:46,420
in the New Yorker Hotel.

657
00:37:46,520 --> 00:37:50,400
Today, room 3327, one of his rooms,

658
00:37:50,401 --> 00:37:53,300
shows the cramped space he occupied for the last 10 years

659
00:37:53,400 --> 00:37:55,500
of his life.

660
00:37:55,600 --> 00:37:59,440
TESLA DIED ALONE IN HIS ROOM AT THE HOTEL.

661
00:37:59,540 --> 00:38:02,080
THEY WEREN'T EVEN SURE EXACTLY WHEN HE DIED

662
00:38:02,180 --> 00:38:04,620
BECAUSE PEOPLE HADN'T SEEN HIM FOR A COUPLE OF DAYS.

663
00:38:04,720 --> 00:38:10,120
A CHAMBERMAID FINALLY ENTERED HIS ROOM THE DAY AFTER HE DIED

664
00:38:10,220 --> 00:38:11,960
AND FOUND HIS BODY.

665
00:38:12,060 --> 00:38:16,060
BUT WHEN HIS DEATH AT AGE 86 WAS DISCOVERED,

666
00:38:16,160 --> 00:38:18,260
THE UNITED STATES GOVERNMENT TOOK CONTROL

667
00:38:18,261 --> 00:38:21,460
of his scientific papers.

668
00:38:21,560 --> 00:38:25,200
There was a great deal of concern at the time of his death

669
00:38:25,300 --> 00:38:27,500
as to what was in his papers, because we were in the middle

670
00:38:27,600 --> 00:38:29,400
of World War II, and there was a great deal of concern.

671
00:38:29,500 --> 00:38:32,700
What if the Nazis get a hold of us?

672
00:38:32,800 --> 00:38:34,740
The government claims that after inspecting

673
00:38:34,840 --> 00:38:38,620
Tesla's papers, they were released in 1952

674
00:38:38,720 --> 00:38:42,360
and later sent to Belgrade, Yugoslavia, where they now

675
00:38:42,460 --> 00:38:46,660
remain at the Nikola Tesla Museum.

676
00:38:46,661 --> 00:38:52,560
However, many believe some of his papers are still missing.

677
00:38:52,660 --> 00:38:54,440
Who knows what were in these papers?

678
00:38:54,540 --> 00:38:55,940
You know, maybe they are very sensitive.

679
00:38:56,040 --> 00:38:57,840
Maybe they are the plans for the death ray.

680
00:38:57,940 --> 00:39:00,980
And, you know, I guess that shouldn't be public knowledge.

681
00:39:01,080 --> 00:39:04,980
It's a terrific mystery who took Nikola Tesla's papers.

682
00:39:05,080 --> 00:39:09,320
We may never know what all these papers contained.

683
00:39:09,420 --> 00:39:12,960
We do know, as his work is being reevaluated,

684
00:39:12,961 --> 00:39:17,140
evaluated that some of his ideas were a century ahead of his time.

685
00:39:20,360 --> 00:39:23,700
In fact, more than 100 years ago,

686
00:39:23,701 --> 00:39:28,240
Tesla was already green.

687
00:39:28,241 --> 00:39:32,820
In 1887, Tesla experimented with X-ray radiation.

688
00:39:32,821 --> 00:39:35,440
That was eight years before Wilhelm Röntgen

689
00:39:35,441 --> 00:39:39,180
documented his own X-ray discovery, which won Röntgen

690
00:39:39,181 --> 00:39:41,420
the Nobel Prize in physics.

691
00:39:41,421 --> 00:39:45,720
Mad Electricity will return on Modern Marvels, here on History.

692
00:39:50,220 --> 00:39:54,060
We now return to Mad Electricity on Modern Marvels.

693
00:39:58,840 --> 00:40:03,000
The United States consumes 20 million barrels of oil a day,

694
00:40:03,520 --> 00:40:06,840
and gasoline prices constantly reach new highs.

695
00:40:06,841 --> 00:40:11,840
A century ago, Tesla saw the future of fossil fuels.

696
00:40:14,020 --> 00:40:16,480
If we use fuel to get our power,

697
00:40:16,580 --> 00:40:20,080
we are living on our capital and exhausting it rapidly.

698
00:40:20,180 --> 00:40:23,220
This method is barbarous and wantonly wasteful

699
00:40:23,320 --> 00:40:25,500
and will have to be stopped in the interest

700
00:40:25,600 --> 00:40:28,600
of coming generations.

701
00:40:28,700 --> 00:40:33,460
Tesla was disturbed by the rate at which society

702
00:40:33,461 --> 00:40:36,900
Society was burning up non-renewable fuels.

703
00:40:37,000 --> 00:40:39,460
Coal, oil was just coming in then.

704
00:40:39,560 --> 00:40:42,600
And he foresaw a time that you would need to go

705
00:40:42,700 --> 00:40:46,740
to some other source of power.

706
00:40:46,840 --> 00:40:49,280
Tesla anticipated the development of what we now

707
00:40:49,380 --> 00:40:51,720
call renewable energy.

708
00:40:51,820 --> 00:40:54,940
In 1901, he patented an apparatus

709
00:40:55,040 --> 00:40:58,980
for the utilization of radiant energy.

710
00:40:59,080 --> 00:41:02,220
The patent refers to the sun, as well as other sources

711
00:41:02,221 --> 00:41:05,960
of radiant energy, like cosmic rays.

712
00:41:06,060 --> 00:41:08,860
Nikola Tesla was one of the first people to realize

713
00:41:08,960 --> 00:41:12,060
that we have an almost endless supply of energy coming in

714
00:41:12,160 --> 00:41:14,360
from space, from our own sun.

715
00:41:14,460 --> 00:41:16,840
I bet he wouldn't be surprised to find out that today

716
00:41:16,940 --> 00:41:21,080
all kinds of applications are running on solar power.

717
00:41:21,180 --> 00:41:24,340
Tesla also championed geothermal power.

718
00:41:24,440 --> 00:41:27,480
In 1931, he wrote an article for The New York Times

719
00:41:27,580 --> 00:41:30,780
entitled Our Future Mode of Power,

720
00:41:30,781 --> 00:41:35,560
which explored how to utilize the Earth's natural energy.

721
00:41:35,660 --> 00:41:39,320
All that is necessary is to find an economic and speedy

722
00:41:39,420 --> 00:41:42,320
way of sinking deep shafts to tap

723
00:41:42,420 --> 00:41:45,620
into this enormous geothermal energy.

724
00:41:50,600 --> 00:41:53,100
One cutting edge car maker is paying homage

725
00:41:53,200 --> 00:41:56,040
to Tesla's green foresight by adopting his name.

726
00:41:56,041 --> 00:41:56,940
name.

727
00:41:59,540 --> 00:42:02,440
Tesla Motors in San Carlos, California,

728
00:42:02,441 --> 00:42:06,280
makes a battery driven Tesla Roadster that goes from 0

729
00:42:06,281 --> 00:42:09,480
to 60 miles per hour in 3.9 seconds.

730
00:42:12,280 --> 00:42:16,660
Using a version of Tesla's AC induction motor,

731
00:42:16,661 --> 00:42:19,700
it travels more than 200 miles without a drop of gas.

732
00:42:19,701 --> 00:42:26,700
Tesla was also a green lighting pioneer.

733
00:42:26,800 --> 00:42:30,540
At the 1893 Chicago World's Fair,

734
00:42:30,640 --> 00:42:32,940
Tesla displayed a fluorescent bulb

735
00:42:33,040 --> 00:42:35,240
that burned much cooler and lasted longer

736
00:42:35,340 --> 00:42:39,320
than Edison's incandescent bulbs, a forerunner

737
00:42:39,420 --> 00:42:42,060
of today's longer-lasting, efficient lighting.

738
00:42:42,160 --> 00:42:45,660
This is an actual Edison light bulb.

739
00:42:45,760 --> 00:42:48,600
This filament's made of carbon.

740
00:42:48,601 --> 00:42:51,840
carbon, and by passing electricity through it,

741
00:42:51,841 --> 00:42:57,340
it becomes heated, and this heat produces a white light.

742
00:42:57,341 --> 00:42:59,980
More than half of the energy that went into this light bulb

743
00:42:59,981 --> 00:43:02,220
produced heat instead of light, and that

744
00:43:02,221 --> 00:43:04,140
made it very inefficient.

745
00:43:04,141 --> 00:43:07,020
Tesla, as a result, developed a lamp

746
00:43:07,021 --> 00:43:09,860
on a different principle that didn't produce heat.

747
00:43:09,861 --> 00:43:13,000
It produced light by a gas inside of a bulb.

748
00:43:13,001 --> 00:43:15,460
The electricity enters the bulb through the ends,

749
00:43:15,461 --> 00:43:17,360
and there's electrodes inside.

750
00:43:17,361 --> 00:43:20,420
And as it enters the bulb, the gas would get excited

751
00:43:20,421 --> 00:43:23,860
and ignite inside, lighting the whole space

752
00:43:23,861 --> 00:43:25,100
within the walls of the tube.

753
00:43:29,040 --> 00:43:33,980
The result was the light was brighter, it ran cooler,

754
00:43:33,981 --> 00:43:36,280
and it was more efficient than Edison's bulb.

755
00:43:36,281 --> 00:43:38,140
The modern compact fluorescent light

756
00:43:38,141 --> 00:43:39,920
operates on the same principle.

757
00:43:39,921 --> 00:43:42,920
There's gas inside that's excited by a high voltage.

758
00:43:42,921 --> 00:43:47,920
And this is Tesla's principle still used today.

759
00:43:47,921 --> 00:43:49,920
Which other of Tesla's ideas

760
00:43:49,921 --> 00:43:52,560
were too far ahead of his time?

761
00:43:52,561 --> 00:43:56,260
History may have neglected this genius,

762
00:43:56,261 --> 00:43:59,600
but today he is being rediscovered.

763
00:43:59,601 --> 00:44:03,600
I believe that someday we will reinvestigate Tesla's work,

764
00:44:03,601 --> 00:44:05,280
and we will discover the secrets

765
00:44:05,281 --> 00:44:07,920
that he was so confident in.

766
00:44:12,920 --> 00:44:16,260
Nikola Tesla made the modern world what it is today.

767
00:44:16,360 --> 00:44:19,120
And although it took many years for a lot of people

768
00:44:19,220 --> 00:44:23,500
to finally realize it, I think perhaps that is changing now.

769
00:44:23,600 --> 00:44:26,360
The legacy of Tesla is really this creative spirit

770
00:44:26,460 --> 00:44:27,660
of the inventor.

771
00:44:27,760 --> 00:44:31,700
To have no bounds on thought, he really

772
00:44:31,800 --> 00:44:34,440
believed anything was possible.

773
00:44:34,540 --> 00:44:38,340
Let the future tell the truth and evaluate each one

774
00:44:38,440 --> 00:44:41,640
according to their work and accomplishments.

775
00:44:41,641 --> 00:44:43,440
The present is theirs.

776
00:44:43,540 --> 00:44:47,780
The future, for which I really worked, is mine.

777
00:44:47,880 --> 00:44:49,080
Nikola Tesla.

